module

public import Rule30Open.Columns.WordTraps

/-!
# Traps for 53 blocks of period 13, from a wall of period thirteen carried leftward

The natural scan of periods 13 and 14 finds that the commonest traps of 16 cells at period 13, of 213, 5,088 and 13,904
states, are walls: the closures of the true windows of the white right half driven by `0001110010011`, `0000000000001`
and `0000000000011` at step 12,000, whose chains become periodic in the distance, all three in the background of 689
cells. Carried leftward up to 53 columns, the first gives traps of 213 states to 53 blocks of period 13 that the kit
does not exclude, every block of period 13 of that background, which the kernel checks one declaration each; the other
two, whose carried traps would take about 4 MB of tables, are left out.

* `th<wall>_<distance>`, `th<wall>_<distance>_check`: the traps, each passing the check of [WordTraps](WordTraps.md).
* `thirteenTraps`, `thirteenTraps_check`, `thirteenTraps_blocks`: the list, its check and its blocks.
* `thirteen_absorption_excludes`: for each of them, if every finitely supported right half driven by the block enters
  its trap, the center column does not eventually repeat the block.

The tables come from `python -m computations.lock_chain --lean-walls 13`. The natural-language argument is
`ThirteenTraps.md`, beside this file.
-/

@[expose] public section

-- Sequential elaboration: the kernel checks would otherwise run in parallel and stack their memory.
set_option Elab.async false

namespace Rule30Open

